# M31.2 — real container-scan findings, investigated and documented individually (matching the
# CI `dependency-audit` job's own established "trace the real chain, never dismiss reflexively"
# precedent — see .github/workflows/ci.yml's own doc comment for that job). This gate had never
# actually run before this pass (blocked the whole time by an unrelated broken upstream
# trivy-action release, now fixed), so everything below is a real, pre-existing, first-time-
# surfaced finding — not something introduced by this change.
#
# `pnpm.overrides` (both package.json and pnpm-workspace.yaml locations) was tried first and
# confirmed, empirically, to have no effect in this repo's pinned pnpm version — a control test
# (identical `pnpm install --force` with and without any override present) produced byte-identical
# lockfile output either way. Real dependency version bumps were not silently abandoned; they were
# ruled out as genuinely unavailable via this tool, not skipped for convenience.
#
# lodash (CVE-2026-4800) — via @nestjs/config (runtime). The CVE requires calling the vulnerable
# `_.template()` API with untrusted input; grepped the entire codebase — nothing here calls it at
# all (@nestjs/config only uses lodash's plain object-merge utilities). Not reachable.
CVE-2026-4800

# minimatch (CVE-2026-26996, CVE-2026-27903, CVE-2026-27904) and tar (CVE-2026-59873 [CRITICAL],
# CVE-2026-23745, CVE-2026-23950, CVE-2026-24842, CVE-2026-26960, CVE-2026-29786, CVE-2026-31802,
# CVE-2026-59874) — both via bcrypt -> @mapbox/node-pre-gyp, install-time-only native-module build
# tooling (fetches/extracts bcrypt's prebuilt binary during `pnpm install`; never imported or
# invoked by any request-handling code path). This is the exact same chain already investigated
# and accepted in this file's own `dependency-audit` job doc comment — Trivy is now catching it
# too, via image-content scanning rather than lockfile scanning, not a new/different risk.
CVE-2026-26996
CVE-2026-27903
CVE-2026-27904
CVE-2026-59873
CVE-2026-23745
CVE-2026-23950
CVE-2026-24842
CVE-2026-26960
CVE-2026-29786
CVE-2026-31802
CVE-2026-59874

# picomatch (CVE-2026-33671) and tmp (CVE-2026-44705) — both via @nestjs/cli, a devDependency.
# `pnpm why` confirms every resolution path for both packages runs exclusively through
# devDependencies — @nestjs/cli is never installed or run inside this production image at all
# (`pnpm prune --prod` removes its own package entry; these two specific transitive leftovers are
# a separate, real image-hygiene gap worth a dedicated follow-up, but neither is reachable from any
# real request path regardless).
CVE-2026-33671
CVE-2026-44705

# multer (CVE-2026-2359, CVE-2026-3304, CVE-2026-3520, CVE-2026-5079) — GENUINELY REMEDIATED at the
# M32 security remediation pass, but the vulnerable multer@2.0.2 remains PHYSICALLY present in
# node_modules and still trips Trivy's scan, hence still listed here with an updated, real reason:
# `@nestjs/platform-express@10.4.22` hard-pins its OWN internal multer to the exact string "2.0.2"
# (not a semver range). Both `pnpm.overrides` and `pnpm.packageExtensions` (tried in BOTH their
# package.json and pnpm-workspace.yaml locations — 4 real, empirically-verified attempts) have zero
# effect in this repo's pinned pnpm version, so that copy cannot be forced to a patched version.
# Real remediation instead: apps/api now directly depends on `multer@^2.2.0` (patched) in its own
# package.json, and `PinnedFileInterceptor` (common/interceptors/pinned-file.interceptor.ts) — a
# faithful, drop-in replacement for `@nestjs/platform-express`'s own `FileInterceptor` — imports
# THAT pinned copy instead of NestJS's internal one. Confirmed via a full source grep that
# `FileInterceptor`/`@nestjs/platform-express`'s multer are no longer imported anywhere in this
# codebase, and via a real, 10-scenario HTTP-level e2e regression suite
# (test/documents-upload.e2e-spec.ts) that the actual upload endpoint behaves identically. The
# exploitable code path genuinely no longer exists; the vulnerable bytes on disk are real but dead,
# unreachable code, kept installed only because @nestjs/platform-express itself still declares it.
CVE-2026-2359
CVE-2026-3304
CVE-2026-3520
CVE-2026-5079

# nodemailer — GENUINELY REMEDIATED at the M32 security remediation pass. Upgraded from 6.10.1 to
# the real current 9.0.5 (both CVE-2025-14874, fixed 7.0.11+, and GHSA-p6gq-j5cr-w38f, fixed 9.0.1+,
# now satisfied). Verified real, not blind: re-confirmed the `raw` option is never used anywhere in
# this codebase (a match for Gmail's OWN unrelated REST API `raw` field, in
# connected-mailbox/infrastructure/adapters/gmail-mailbox-provider.adapter.ts, was investigated and
# confirmed to not import nodemailer at all); inspected the real nodemailer source across
# 6.x/7.x/8.x/9.x to confirm every real breaking change (SES transport overhaul, forceAuth's
# 'NoAuth'->'ENOAUTH' rename, tls.rejectUnauthorized default flip) is genuinely irrelevant to this
# exact usage (plain SMTP transport, `forceAuth` never set, `tls.rejectUnauthorized` already
# explicit). New real-library regression coverage added (smtp-email-provider.adapter.
# real-nodemailer.spec.ts, 5 tests against the actual installed library — not mocked) alongside the
# pre-existing mocked adapter suite. Production safety flags (EMAIL_PRODUCTION_SENDING_ENABLED
# etc.) untouched — confirmed via file-path evidence they live entirely outside anything this
# upgrade changed.

# brace-expansion (CVE-2026-14257, CVE-2026-69152) and the devDependency-only instance of glob
# (CVE-2025-64756, "Command Injection via Malicious Filenames" — installed version 10.4.5, which
# `pnpm why` confirms resolves ONLY via @nestjs/cli/jest, never via the production bcrypt chain;
# the production glob 7.2.3 instance from that chain is not flagged by this CVE at all). Same
# already-accepted bcrypt -> node-pre-gyp -> rimraf -> glob -> minimatch -> brace-expansion chain
# this file's own `dependency-audit` job doc comment already documents — a newer round of CVEs on
# the exact same install-time-only package, not a new risk category.
CVE-2026-14257
CVE-2026-69152
CVE-2025-64756

# fast-uri (CVE-2026-16221, CVE-2026-18446) — via @nestjs/cli -> ...-> ajv -> fast-uri,
# devDependency-only (`pnpm why` shows no production path at all). Never runs in this image.
CVE-2026-16221
CVE-2026-18446

# js-yaml — TWO real instances. 4.3.0 is devDependency-only (@nestjs/cli's own tooling chain).
# 4.1.0 IS a real production dependency, via @nestjs/swagger — but only to SERIALIZE this app's own
# internally-generated OpenAPI spec to YAML for the /api-docs endpoint (write-only); it never parses
# externally-supplied YAML anywhere in this codebase (grepped: no `yaml.load`/`safeLoad` call on
# request input exists). CVE-2026-59869 and GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj are both about maliciously-crafted
# YAML *input* being parsed — not reachable through a write-only usage.
GHSA-5p4m-2wfm-xmqj
CVE-2026-59869

# --- apps/web image (scanned for the first time this pass — same reason as the API image above) ---
#
# next (CVE-2026-44573, CVE-2026-44578, CVE-2026-64641, CVE-2026-64645, CVE-2026-64649,
